msgid "Connect Mission Planner to AutoPilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:7
#: ebc4967f83e34300ad2f1daa78274acf
msgid "This article explains how to connect *Mission Planner* to an autopilot in order to receive telemetry and control the vehicle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:12
#: 0832eb21befe4e1ab0a54a132c2f0a8e
msgid "There are separate instructions for connecting in order to :ref:`Load Firmware <common-loading-firmware-onto-pixhawk>` for existing ArduPilot firmware installations, or for boards :ref:`without existing ArduPilot firmware <common-loading-firmware-onto-chibios-only-boards>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:16
#: 9be6c89c75d04a6f8c21dda4aef966b4
msgid "Setting up the connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:18
#: c9e6b687d5104ddaa1a6d646929ba29a
msgid "To establish a connection you must first choose the communication method/channel you want to use, and then set up the physical hardware and Windows device drivers. You can connect the PC and autopilot using USB cables, :ref:`Telemetry Radios <copter:common-telemetry-landingpage>`, :ref:`Bluetooth <common-mission-planner-bluetooth-connectivity_detailed_connecting_with_mission_planner>`, IP connections etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:27
#: a872b8b360e54183a87caf32c2e28ae1
msgid "The driver for your connection hardware must be present on Windows as this makes your connection's COM port and default data rate available to *Mission Planner*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:35
#: d66bcf559a3343c793b0b78edc5a34c6
msgid "Pixhawk USB Connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:41
#: 5606d5c00f5746039968c345440967cf
msgid "Connection using SiK Radio"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:43
#: bd59907bf5164dffb3d109f520a2f36d
msgid "On *Mission Planner*, the connection and data rate are set up using the drop down boxes in the upper right portion of the screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:49
#: c774c6731aed44b3933d1314fa4cf7e9
msgid "Once you've attached the USB or Telemetry Radio, Windows will automatically assign your autopilot a COM port number, and that will show in the drop-down menu (the actual number does not matter). The appropriate data rate for the connection is also set (typically the USB connection data rate is 115200 and the radio connection rate is 57600)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:55
#: e77820342673467e8bee6b24340a26ab
msgid "Select the desired port and data rate and then press the **CONNECT** button to connect to the autopilot. After connecting **Mission Planner** will download parameters from the autopilot and the button will change to **DISCONNECT** as shown:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:65
#: b6712a4a90e14efebb0a6f6092c4670c
msgid "The \"select port\" dropdown also contains TCP or UDP port options that can be used to connect to an autopilot over a network."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:68
#: 96a1ec69d8204223ac219c0600d2d827
msgid "The \"Stats...\" hotlink beneath the port selection box, if clicked, will give information about the connection, such as if :ref:`Signing security<common-MAVLink2-signing>` is active, link stats, etc. Sometimes this window pops up beneath the current screen and will have to be brought to the front to be seen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:74
#: e6951ed7ac7d4f7eace619672f416eee
msgid "Connecting to multiple vehicles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:75
#: 687ab81e211a4b2bb6d259cd601ce2d2
msgid "Additional connections can be made by right-clicking the **CONNECT** button and selecting **Connection Options** from the drop-down list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:80
#: f01bc7e46c5b49fe9ea6373093c887d4
msgid "A file with a pre-written list of connections can be loaded with the **Connection List** drop-down option. This is an example format of the file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:90
#: 252635d1e38b4c8f8cf237d9f256d9fd
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:92
#: 7c27963e104646f1a7654b8a4f5c4b4a
msgid "If Mission Planner is unable to connect:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:94
#: 9f173f782c824d6dadc8267d116f8235
msgid "Check that the correct baud rate is used for the selected method (115200 on USB or 57600 on Radio/Telemetry)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:96
#: 626845e4e9e7459a981a552153842159
msgid "If attaching via USB, be sure that a few seconds after power up have passed before attempting to connect. If you attempted to connect during the bootloader initialization time, Windows may get the wrong USB information. Connection attempts after this may require that the USB connection be  unplugged and re-plugged,then wait for bootloader to enter the main code ( few seconds), then attempt the connection. Occasionally, MP must be restarted if an attempt to connect is made while in the bootloader initialization period."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:97
#: aaba8ac693254b96809bd21742a3f4e2
msgid "If using a COM port on Windows, check that the connection's COM port exists in the Windows Device Manager's list of serial ports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:99
#: 2f27168a4a1341d383ce5617ebeef701
msgid "If your autopilot has an F7 or H7 processor and has CAN ports, then see the section below, :ref:`Troubleshooting Composite Connections <troubleshooting-composite-connections>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:100
#: 9107c619b4764efa8a1def3ee04f5af6
msgid "If using a USB port, try a different physical USB port"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:101
#: aa81ec3a02bd4223a9cde9dd5bf0c5d2
msgid "If using a UDP or TCP connection, check that your firewall is not blocking IP traffic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:103
#: 28ab3459a8f142b9b3d5044b855f191c
msgid "You should also ensure that the autopilot controller board has appropriate ArduPilot firmware installed and has booted correctly (on Pixhawk there are useful :ref:`LEDs <common-leds-pixhawk>` and :ref:`Sounds <common-sounds-pixhawkpx4>` which can tell you the state of the autopilot)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:108
#: d11748ba86ca4619957d5b2cf0aba33a
msgid "If using a remote link (not USB) and Mission Planner connects, but does not download parameters or you cannot get commands, like mode changes acted upon,then the autopilot probably has Signing turned on. See :ref:`common-MAVLink2-signing`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:113
#: 6d7edd4dca7f425d93979e7eeb34e8e8
msgid "Troubleshooting Composite Connections"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:115
#: 0d788936b66b48cbaba0a3e9bb505ddb
msgid "Autopilots with F7 or H7 processors and having CAN interfaces use firmware that presents two USB interfaces: One for the normal MAVLink connection, and one for SLCAN serial connections to the CAN interface for configuration and firmware updates.This is called a composite USB device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:117
#: 40ea2cc4d0154fde92bea51c6cda91e8
msgid "By default, the MAVLink USB interface is SERIAL0 and the SLCAN USB interface is the highest SERIALx port the board presents. The Windows driver currently installed with Mission Planner may select to use either one, and since both are set by default in ArduPilot firmware for MAVLINK protocol, it will work fine, whichever one it chooses as the COM port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:119
#: b50d54c515f44279a6a62bd97195775e
msgid "However, there is a situation where the user will find that it will not connect to the obvious COM port in the Mission Planner dropdown box.This occurs when the user accidentally changes the protocol of whichever SERIALx port the Windows driver is using as the MAVLink COM port to something other than MAVLink. This can easily happen if the user takes an existing parameter file from a vehicle configuration used with a different autopilot that has the protocol changed. For example, the user has a plane with non F7/H7 CAN capable autopilot and upgrades it to one that is, then loads his existing parameter file while setting up the plane with the new autopilot. As soon as the parameter file is loaded and the autopilot is rebooted, communication is lost and cannot be re-established."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:121
#: 58bdd718ddba4783861e2a0faf536343
msgid "What has occurred, is that the protocol for the SERIALx port that Windows was using has been changed. Almost always, this is the highest numbered SERIALx port since that is commonly set to -1 on non-CAN capable autopilots, and the Windows COM port driver has selected this interface as the COM port instead of SERIAL0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:123
#: 3220d206685d4fd0807739e6ba7c5b65
msgid "The procedure to recover is as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:127
#: c68eefafe86c4877883979e170ee9388
msgid "Go to Windows Device Manager and find the COM port being used by the autopilot in the Ports listings. It will have the COM Port # you used to connect initially to Mission Planner. Right click and it will present \"Update driver software\" as one of the options. Click it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:131
#: 941bd25689044102ae32dae2ce3917f2
msgid "Click the \"Browse my computer......\" option and then click the \"Choose from a list...\" option and you will see this screen:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:135
#: b250c61e9874430ea522bcfe464a1a2b
msgid "Scroll down the top list until \"Composite USB\" option appears and click it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:137
#: 70c36d5f415a43a2bdfef879b84fae6a
msgid "Now reconnect your autopilot to the PC and two COM ports will be presented. One will connect (the remaining one with MAVLink Protocol) and the other will not. If you do not connect to one, try the other. But DO NOT disconnect the autopilot from the PC or the composite driver will unload and you will have to start over."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-connect-mission-planner-autopilot.rst:139
#: a671684663154c018942b2fadf07882c
msgid "Now that you are connected to Mission Planner, change back the protocol of the Serialx port protocol to 2 (MAVLink2). You can now disconnect and reconnect the autopilot and it will present only one COM port and you should be able to connect from now on. Do not change this protocol from now on unless trying to utilize the SLCAN interface. It may be a bit unfamiliar since the Mission Planner SERIALx port being used is no longer the normal SERIAL0 but rather,the highest port, but this does not affect anything in the autopilot's configuration and operation."
msgstr ""

